# Video trim lock (do not regress)

Trimming a 21s file to 00:09 used native `<video controls>`, which still showed **00:21**. Pausing by seeking to `clipEnd` looped the buffering spinner. `npm test` must stay green (`src/lib/floatingMedia.test.ts`).

## Invariants (code, not comments)

- **Clip is a playback window**, not an ffmpeg re-encode. `clipStart` / `clipEnd` on [`FloatingImage`](src/types/course.ts). Helpers live in [`src/lib/floatingMedia.ts`](src/lib/floatingMedia.ts) (`videoClipRange`, `clipDisplayTime`, `formatVideoClock`).
- **Seekbar matches the trim.** When `clipped` is true, [`FloatingVideo`](src/components/FloatingImageCanvas.tsx) hides native controls and shows a clip-relative bar (`00:09 / 00:09`). Untrimmed videos keep native controls.
- **Do not seek at clip end.** Pause once; do **not** assign `currentTime = clipEnd` on every `timeupdate` — that seek is the spinner loop. Play after the clip restarts at `clipStart`.
- **Move/resize stays.** Authoring drag bar, always-on handles, `ignoreFrom: 'button, .float-handle, .float-video-controls'`. Do not put `ignoreFrom: 'video'` back.

## How to change this

Change `videoClipRange` / `FloatingVideo` and add a failing test first. Native controls cannot fake a shorter `duration`.
